# REST resources

The path is the API's public vocabulary. Clients, docs and logs repeat it for years, so name it from the domain before writing a handler. If you can't say what the resource is in one noun, the design isn't ready: stop and ask.

The prefix, error envelope, pagination limits and wrong-method behavior this project chose are in AGENTS.md, "API conventions". Examples below use the default `/v1` prefix.

## Naming paths

- A path names a resource, never an action. The method says what happens. `POST /v1/orders`, not `POST /v1/createOrder`, `/v1/orders/create` or `/v1/order-creation`.
- Collections are plural nouns: `/v1/orders`, `/v1/orders/{orderId}`. Never mix `/v1/order/{id}` and `/v1/orders`.
- Lowercase kebab-case segments (`/v1/line-items`), camelCase JSON fields (`createdAt`). No underscores, file extensions or trailing slashes in paths.
- Use the domain's word, the one users and the code already use, consistently. Don't alternate synonyms (`users`, `accounts`, `members`) for one thing, and don't invent a new noun when an existing resource fits. Search the API definitions for the existing name first.
- Path params are named for their resource (`{orderId}`, not `{id}` or `{param}`) and identify exactly one thing. Everything optional (filters, sort, paging) goes in the query.
- Nest only for ownership, and one level deep: `/v1/orders/{orderId}/line-items`. A resource that exists on its own gets its own top-level collection, filtered by query (`/v1/line-items?orderId=...`), not `/v1/customers/{c}/orders/{o}/line-items`.
- No verbs, no `get`, `list`, `do`, `handle`, `manage`, `data`, `info` or `api` in paths. Generic nouns like `items`, `records` or `objects` name nothing.
- An operation that truly isn't CRUD becomes a sub-resource noun for its outcome: `POST /v1/orders/{orderId}/cancellation`, `POST /v1/exports` (which returns an export resource you can GET). Reach for this only after trying a field update (`PATCH status`).
- Operation names in code follow one pattern per resource: `list`, `get`, `create`, `update`, `delete`, then a noun for any sub-resource. The module or file is named for the plural path (`orders`).

## Methods and status codes

| Method | Use | Success |
|---|---|---|
| `GET /things` | List, paginated | 200 with the page |
| `GET /things/{thingId}` | Read one | 200, or 404 |
| `POST /things` | Create; the server assigns the id | 201 with the created resource and a `Location` header |
| `PUT /things/{thingId}` | Replace the whole resource, only when the client owns the id | 200 or 201 |
| `PATCH /things/{thingId}` | Partial update of named fields | 200 with the updated resource |
| `DELETE /things/{thingId}` | Remove | 204; deleting an absent resource is 404 |

GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent and GET changes nothing; never put a side effect behind GET. Status codes come only from the project's error table: 400 malformed or invalid input, 401 no or bad credentials, 403 authenticated but not allowed, 404 absent (also for a resource the caller may not know exists), 409 conflict with current state (a duplicate unique key, a stale version), 415 a body that isn't JSON (only where the stack checks content type), 422 only if the API already distinguishes well-formed-but-invalid from malformed, 429 with `Retry-After`, 503 dependency down. A wrong method on a declared path gets what AGENTS.md records (405 with `Allow`, or the 404 envelope); test that, not the other. A 405 always carries `Allow` (RFC 9110); a 405 without it is never acceptable. Don't return 200 with an error inside the body.

## Bodies

- Requests and responses are JSON objects at the top level, never bare arrays or strings, so a field can be added later without breaking clients.
- A resource has the same shape everywhere it appears: the create response, the read and each list item are one schema. Ids are opaque strings, timestamps are ISO 8601 UTC strings named `<event>At`, money is integer minor units plus a currency.
- Responses never expose storage details: no column names that differ from the domain word, no internal ids, no stack traces. Decode input and encode output through declared schemas.
- Absent optional fields are omitted, not `null`, unless null means something different from absent.

## Lists

- Every list is paginated from its first version, with a server-side maximum page size. Cursor pagination by default: `?limit=50&cursor=<opaque>`, responding `{ "items": [...], "nextCursor": "<opaque>" | omitted }`. Offset pagination only for small, rarely changing collections.
- Filters are query params named after the field (`?status=open&customerId=...`); sort is `?sort=createdAt` or `?sort=-createdAt`.
- Reject unknown query params with 400 rather than ignoring them. Configure that for the query only: rejecting unknown headers too breaks every real request, because clients and proxies add headers.
- Order is deterministic: sort ties break on the id.

## Versions and change

- Resources live under the version prefix; health checks and the OpenAPI document don't. Adding an optional field or a new endpoint is compatible. Renaming, removing or retyping a field, changing a status code, or making an optional input required is breaking and needs a new version or a deprecation period, so name things right the first time.
- The generated OpenAPI document is the contract. Review its diff when an endpoint changes; a path, param or schema name you wouldn't want in public docs is wrong in the code too.

## Request boundary

- Every API error response uses the project's one envelope, recorded in AGENTS.md; the default is `{ "error": { "tag", "message", "retryable" } }`. One exhaustive error table owns statuses and retryability, with fixed safe messages. Rewrite framework-generated errors (decode failures, route misses and wrong methods included) into the envelope at the request boundary. Invalid input is 400; response encoding failures and unexpected errors are a generic 500 whose body carries nothing internal.
- Fill a missing `Cache-Control` on API responses with `no-store`; preserve any endpoint policy and leave a 304 alone. A cacheable endpoint declares and tests its own policy and ETag handling.
- Test the envelope and caching behavior through real HTTP, including framework errors and route misses, wherever the stack serves the API.
